.TH SCAN_UTF8_SEM 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
scan_utf8_sem \- Scan a semantically valid UTF-8 character from a string

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/scan.h>

.IB size_t
.IB scan_utf8_sem(const char* in, size_t len, uint32* num);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBscan_utf8_sem\fR function scans a semantically valid UTF-8 character from a given string and stores the result in the location pointed to by \fBnum\fR. The function checks for non-characters and surrogates, and returns 0 if any of these are encountered.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB in
The input string containing the UTF-8 character.
.TP
.IB len
The maximum number of characters to read from the input string.
.TP
.IB num
The destination where the scanned UTF-8 character will be stored.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of characters processed. If a non-character or surrogate is encountered, the function returns 0.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBscan_utf8_sem\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/scan.h>

int main() {
    const char* input = "\xed\xa0\x80";  # Non-character
    size_t len = 4;
    uint32 result;
    size_t processed = scan_utf8_sem(input, len, &result);
    printf("Processed characters: %zu\n", processed);
    return 0;
}
.nef